What are Record Classes?

Record classes are a special kind of class introduced to model immutable data objects with significantly less boilerplate code.

Instead of manually writing:
Fields
Constructor
Getter methods
equals()
hashCode()
toString()

Java generates them automatically.


Why Use Records?
Traditional Java classes require a lot of repetitive code for simple data objects.

Example:

public class Rectangle {

    private final double length;
    private final double width;

    public Rectangle(double length, double width) {
        this.length = length;
        this.width = width;
    }

    public double length() {
        return length;
    }

    public double width() {
        return width;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) { ... }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() { ... }

    @Override
    public String toString() { ... }
}

With Records:

record Rectangle(double length, double width) {}



Syntax
record RecordName(Type field1, Type field2) {
}

Example
record Rectangle(double length, double width) {}

Creating a Record Object
Records are instantiated just like normal classes.
Rectangle rectangle = new Rectangle(4.0, 5.0);



Members Automatically Generated : 

For
record Rectangle(double length, double width) {}

Java automatically creates:

1) Private Final Fields :

private final double length;
private final double width;

Fields are:
private
final

which makes records immutable.

2) Public Accessor Methods

Instead of getters:

rectangle.length();
rectangle.width();

Not

rectangle.getLength();
rectangle.getWidth();


3) Canonical Constructor

Automatically generated constructor

Rectangle rectangle = new Rectangle(4, 5);

Equivalent to

public Rectangle(double length, double width) {
    this.length = length;
    this.width = width;
}

4) equals()

Two record objects are equal if:

They are of the same type.
Their component values are equal.

Example

Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle(4,5);
Rectangle r2 = new Rectangle(4,5);

System.out.println(r1.equals(r2));

Output

true

5) hashCode()

Automatically generated based on record components.

System.out.println(r1.hashCode());
System.out.println(r2.hashCode());

Equal records produce identical hash codes.

6) toString()

Automatically generated.

System.out.println(r1);

Output:
Rectangle[length=4.0, width=5.0]
